+/*
+ * VMark patch (#1473): sibling indices without an `indexOf` per text node.
+ *
+ * For every text node, `findAndReplace` looked up each ancestor's index among
+ * its siblings (to pass to the `ignore` test) and the text node's own (to
+ * splice), each with an `indexOf` over the whole children array: O(text nodes
+ * × top-level blocks) on a long flat document, O(siblings²) in a paragraph of
+ * many inline nodes. The answers are unchanged:
+ *
+ * - An ancestor's index is only ever passed to `ignore`, and a type, property
+ *   or `null` test never reads it; only a test function can. So it is computed
+ *   — with `indexOf`, as before — exactly when the test contains a function,
+ *   and left `undefined` otherwise.
+ * - A text node's own index: while its siblings are distinct objects, the one
+ *   index holding it IS `indexOf`'s answer, and it is found by looking outward
+ *   from where the last one was — the traversal moves forward, so that is
+ *   usually a step or two. That shortcut is taken only for an array checked
+ *   to be distinct since caller code last ran: caller code may edit the tree,
+ *   so after it an array seen before goes back to `indexOf` for the rest of
+ *   the call. Caller code is every replace function; the `ignore` test when
+ *   it holds a function or a property test with an accessor; and a pattern
+ *   whose `exec` or `global` is not the built-in one (as they were when this
+ *   module loaded). Every splice here follows a replace call, so it needs no
+ *   tracking of its own. The distinctness check itself changes an answer
+ *   only where the unpatched code never ends (a text node without a match,
+ *   reached twice among the same siblings), and keeps that the same too.
+ *
+ * Nodes are taken to be plain data: unist requires every value in a tree to
+ * be "expressible in JSON values", so a node whose getter runs code is not a
+ * tree this function is defined on.
+ */
+
+const own = {}.hasOwnProperty
+const builtInExec = RegExp.prototype.exec
+const builtInGlobal = /** @type {PropertyDescriptor} */ (
+  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(RegExp.prototype, 'global')
+).get
